A||||2||Brian84SE||Emissions update III||||1||
Z||000000||Brian84SE||04-03-2000||08:21 PM||||For those of you who have followed my emissions problems (went through six times before finally passing) thanks for all your help!  To refresh everyone, I was suffering from high NOx.(yes, the egr was working).<P>As it turned out, it was the catalytic converter.  It brought the NOx from 1900 ppm to 355 ppm and the HC from 180 ppm to around 35 ppm. The cat really does do something!<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||Patrick||04-04-2000||01:23 AM||||<BR>I've been through the same thing myself. My question is this: <P>Is a catalytic converter necessary on a properly running engine? <P>It's great that the cat burns up so much garbage, but why is there so much to begin with? My '87 2.5L Fiero, which appears to run well, literally pumped raw fuel out of the tailpipe before I changed the cat. However, my '78 Z28 Camaro, with a high performance 350, easily passes the emission test <I>without</I> the supposedly required catalytic converter. Go figure. ||||reg||4
Z||000002||mshill||04-04-2000||02:43 AM||||Brian,  Went through that with my 86SE V6 a few months back.  Did  just about everything and the NOx was OK but the HCs were just barely over.  Put on a new cat and poof, hardly a trace.<P>Patrick, the nine year difference between your two cars is probably why the Camaro passes without a cat.  Different years, different requirements.  I really believe the <BR>V6 engines have to have the cat to pass.||||reg||1
